Remember that the C# Debug.Assert statement checks a condition and if the condition is false, it writes a message to the output window and then throws an exception. Normally, these checks are only executed in debug builds of your application. If you want to disable assertions completely, you can do so by removing the "DEBUG" define in the project settings, or changing the build configuration to release.--------------------The provided code does not contain any comment line starting with "@@@". Hence, there are no output assert statements corresponding to the requirements. The given C# code does not contain any "Debug.Assert" statements either.--------------------```cs

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        [PexMethod]
        public void PUT_ContainsContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, [PexAssumeUnderTest] int x)
        {
            // Setup
            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool New_Ret = arrList.Contains(x);

            // Expected state
            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

            // Assertions
            Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount >= 0);
            Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount);
            Debug.Assert(Old_x == x);
            Debug.Assert(New_Ret == arrList.Contains(x));
            Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX == arrList.IndexOf(x));
            Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX == arrList.IndexOf(x));
            Debug.Assert(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX == arrList.LastIndexOf(x));
            Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX == arrList.LastIndexOf(x));
            Debug.Assert(Math.Max(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX, -1) == arrList.LastIndexOf(x));
        }
    }
}

```
This piece of code is using the Debug.Assert method to assert conditions throughout the method. The assertions are based on the original and expected states of the list as well as other variables. The "@@@ If the return value is false, last position of x in old list is >= -1" comment can be translated to the Assert statement: 

Assert that the maximum between the LastIndexOf x in old list and -1 is equal to the LastIndexOf x in the new list. This means if the return value is false in the original list, the LastIndexOf x in the original and new lists should be the same or -1.--------------------Here is the output for the assert statements in the posted C# code:

